[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you have ever had to work with any API keys, tokens, database passwords, or encryption keys, you\'re most definitely familiar with .env files. A well-known concept aimed to separate your secrets from your code by moving them into a plain text file in a form of environment'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'variables. They\'re pretty simple, supported by practically any languages, and that\'s unfortunately where the tradeoffs begin. Because the moment you introduce a .env file, your project stops being fully self-contained. Our'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'secrets are no longer a proper part of the repository, even though the project depends on them, and our codebase is no longer enough to run the project, now requiring an external artifact that is not versioned, not enforced, and not'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tied to the repository life cycle. What\'s worse, since the file is dangerous to commit, but still lives in the repo\'s directory, we\'re not really decoupling it from the project. In fact, we are just hiding the secret names from'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'new contributors, while doing little to prevent leaks or keep our secrets secure. So, how could we address all these issues? The answer is pretty simple. All we have to do is move the secret values out of the project'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'directory. Keep the secret names in a project file, but place the values in some secure encrypted place, like your system keyring or some service like Bitwarden or LastPass. This would make our file completely safe to commit,'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'making it a proper part of our project, take care of security, since all the sensitive data is now safely stored elsewhere, make secret discovery as straightforward as it can be, all while giving you, the developer, a central'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'place to monitor, rotate, and manage your keys with your secret store of choice, resulting in an ultimate secret management solution that does not just decouple our secrets from our code, but'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more importantly, safely decouples the values from the codebase. And this solution is a free and open source project called Secrets Pack, a straightforward CLI and SDK written in Rust that does exactly what I described.'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let me show you how it works. It all starts in any of your projects which require some secrets. To begin, we of course need to initialize our secrets spec file using the secrets spec in it command, optionally passing it a from'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the end flag in case you have one already. Like I\'ve said earlier, the resulting file declares your secrets by name and also holds some extra options like descriptions, default values, and more. To demonstrate how it works, let\'s'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'define a secret called my secret password and then set its value with the secrets spec set command. It will let you type in its value just like with SSH or sudo, so type it in, click enter, and'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'now we can retrieve the value with the secrets spec get command. And more importantly, run any commands with their secrets present as environment variables with the secrets'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spec run {dash} {dash} your command. As you can see, aside from including the secrets spec file, we don\'t have to modify any project logic at all. This secrets spec files pretty much backwards'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compatible with the end files for any other developers who may enjoy inferior solutions. But this of course is just the beginning, because like I mentioned earlier, your system keyring is not the only place that can be used as your'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'secrets spec provider. And using the {dash} {dash} provider flag, we can select one of more than a dozen providers ranging from something simple like pass to Bitwarden, 1Password, or AWS Secrets Manager. Each provider is'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thoroughly documented on the respective docs page explaining everything from authentication process to required dependencies and naming conventions. By using a specific provider, you can keep'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all your secrets in one place, not only making them easy to manage, but also allowing you to rotate your keys everywhere at once as opposed to just having them scattered around different files, projects, and machines. The only problem, passing a {dash} {dash}'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'provider flag every time you want to run a command is pretty inconvenient. So, instead, we can use the Secret Spec config init command, allowing us to generate a user config file with some preferences we want to use by default.'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It will let us choose the default provider and profiles we want to use and store the preferences in a config file that will live somewhere in your user config directory. And now that we understand how Secret Spec works, let\'s'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'take a look at some of its other features which make it even cooler. The first and most important of which is, of course, its guardrails. Because after the Secret Spec config file is written, it won\'t let you run the project until'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you have the required secrets set and won\'t let you set secrets that were not defined. Not for security reasons, but purely for convenience. Think of data types in strongly typed languages.'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They\'re technically not needed. You may spend a little extra time writing them, but in the long run, they just make your life easier. And with the convenient profile feature, you can also define different rules should follow depending'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the profile. Like in this example, where API key value will be required in the default profile, but come with some default testing value in the development profile. Deployed program will refuse to run until we set our keys, but during'

[05:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'development, we will have some internal default value. But I already hear you say, "All these features are cool and all, but you cannot guarantee that project developers will be using Secret Spec." And to that, I tell you that'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Secret Spec also comes with first-class SDKs for a whole bunch of languages and runtimes, including Python, Ruby, Go, Node, Haskell, Rust, and more, allowing you to resolve the secrets from code,'

[05:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'skipping the environment variables entirely. And in case of Rust, it even lets you use a macro to generate secret structs at compile time, making accessing the secrets type safe as if they really were just variables you'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'defined earlier. With SDKs, your program will, of course, become dependent on Secret working on a project, perhaps this is exactly what you want. From other notable features of secrets back, it\'s probably worth mentioning its'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'secret generation capability, allowing you to generate secret values automatically, the audit logging, ensuring you always know which secrets were accessed, where and when, and of course the dev environment integration, making it super powerful when used with'

